  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts` with the water scene. It passes `typecheck`, but I couldn't run `vite build` or load it in a browser here. I haven't seen a rendered frame, so the look and colours are untested.\n\n- **Water:** a custom GLSL material on a 320×320 grid that is denser near the camera. The vertex shader sums four sine waves for geometry displacement and computes the normal analytically. The fragment shader adds scrolling fbm noise ripples, a Schlick fresnel mix between the water body colour and the reflected sky, and a sun glint (a tight lobe plus a broad sheen). It also adds a little crest foam and fades to the sky colour toward the horizon. Time comes from a `waveClock` uniform set every frame in `app.onFrame`.\n- **Sky:** an inside-out sphere with a custom gradient shader, with a sun glow and sun disc. The water calls the same `skyColor` function for its reflection, so the two match.\n- **Camera and colour:** the camera sits 3 units above the water and looks slightly upward, so the horizon falls just below the middle of the frame. Colours are authored in sRGB and decoded to linear in the shaders. I set `output.toneMapping: \"none\"` because the docs say the default ACES operator greys out custom shader colours. The catch is that very bright glint values clip instead of rolling off.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after `app.ready()` and one animation frame.\n\n",
